==English==
{{was wotd|2012|July|21}}

===Etymology 1===
Late 17th century, of {{unk|en|title=unknown}} origin;

* Possibly related to {{cog|enm|bemothered|t=confused, perplexed}}, itself probably related to {{cog|ang|mēþe|t=exhausting, troublesome}}, {{cog|non|móðr|t=angry, frustrated}}, {{cog|dum|moeden|t=to tire, exhaust, disturb, trouble}}.
* Possibly allied to {{der|en|cy|moedro|t=to worry or bother}}, though according to the GPC, the latter is borrowed from English.<ref>{{R:cy:GPC|moedro}}</ref>
* Alternatively, possibly tied to {{cog|cy|meidda|t=to beg for whey}}, from {{m|cy|maidd|t=whey}}.
* Perhaps from {{cog|cy|meiddio|t=to dare or venture}}, a variant of {{m|cy|beiddio|t=to dare}}.

The "dd" in Welsh corresponds in sound to the "th" in mither, and English also has {{m|en|moider}} and {{m|en|moither}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ˈmaɪðə(ɹ)/}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪðə(ɹ)|s=2}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive|Northern England|especially|_|Manchester}} To make an [[unnecessary]] [[fuss]], [[moan]], [[bother]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|text=The European Commission is pondering how to compel phone companies to come up with a universal plug for their chargers. Apple, which uses its own design for its charger cables, is '''mithering''' about the change.|year=6 February 2020|title=The parable of the plug|journal=The Economist}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|Northern England|especially|_|Manchester}} To [[pester]] or [[irritate]] someone.
#* {{quote-journal|en|text="Every night after work I'd '''mither''' [pester] my dad to take me out into the field and practise. There were definitely times when he didn't want to do it, but he never said no.|year=11 June 2019|title=Women's World Cup: England v Argentina – Keira Walsh 'inspired' by opponents|journal=BBC Sport}}

=====Usage notes=====
* {{sense|pester, irritate}} Usually describes the actions of a child.

